The '''vagina''' ({{plural form}}: '''vaginas''' or '''vaginae''')<ref name="Stevenson">{{cite book|vauthors=Stevenson A|title=Oxford Dictionary of English|publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]|isbn=978-0-19-957112-3|year=2010|page=1962|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=anecAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA1962|access-date=October 27, 2015|archive-date=June 3, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210603161739/https://books.google.com/books?id=anecAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA1962|url-status=live}}</ref> is the elastic, muscular [[sex organ|reproductive organ]] of the [[female]] [[genital tract]] through which [[mammal]]s [[Copulation (zoology)|copulate]] and give [[birth]]. In humans, it extends from the [[vulval vestibule]] to the [[cervix]] (neck of the [[uterus]]). The [[#Vaginal opening and hymen|vaginal introitus]] is often partly covered by a thin layer of [[mucous membrane|mucosal tissue]] called the [[hymen]]. The [[Vaginal epithelium|vaginal tissues]] are highly elastic and flexible in the reproductive years, allowing the vaginal [[Lumen (anatomy)|lumen]] to distend for [[vaginal delivery]]. The vagina’s elasticity and lack of restrictive structures such as [[sphincter]]s and [[Transverse folds of the rectum|valves]] that would limit its distensibility also give rise to [[vaginal flatulence]] as air is continually displaced into and out of the [[vaginal vault]]. The vagina also channels [[Menstruation (mammal)|menstrual flow]], which occurs in humans and closely related [[primate]]s as part of the [[menstrual cycle]].

During [[sexual intercourse]], the vaginal walls produce [[vaginal lubrication]] and create friction which stimulates the [[penis]] toward [[Ejaculated|ejaculation]], enabling [[fertilization]]. Along with pleasure and bonding, [[Human sexual activity|sexual activity]] can cause [[Sexually transmitted disease|sexually transmitted infections]] (STIs), which can be prevented by [[safe sex]] practices. [[#Infections, diseases, and safe sex|Other health issues]] may also affect the human vagina.

The vagina has evoked strong reactions in societies throughout history, including negative perceptions and language, cultural [[taboo]]s, and their use as symbols for [[female sexuality]], spirituality, or regeneration of life. In [[Colloquialism|common speech]], the word "vagina" is often used incorrectly to refer to the [[vulva]] or to the female genitals in general. 
